Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 ms1:{plain:{version:"Every culture offers sacrifice because we know we owe God everything. A sacrifice needs a priest, a gift, God as the one honoured, and something done to the gift. The Old Testament sacrifices were rehearsals for Christ's.",question:"What is a sacrifice, and why do humans offer it?",one:"Sacrifice gives God, by an outward sign, the honour and self-giving we owe him."},
  hook:"Priest · victim · God alone · immolation.",
  analogy:{title:"The ring",text:"A wedding ring is a visible thing that says an invisible promise. Sacrifice is like that: a visible gift that says \"I belong to you\" to God."},
  sort:{title:"Which end of sacrifice?",cats:["Adoration","Thanksgiving","Propitiation","Petition"],items:[["The holocaust burned entirely",0],["The Day of Atonement",2],["A peace offering after a harvest",1],["Samuel's offering before battle",3],["A Mass for a sinner's conversion",2]]}},
 ms2:{plain:{version:"Jesus' death was a real sacrifice, offered freely by Jesus himself, who is both priest and victim. What made it a sacrifice was his love and obedience, not the cruelty of the soldiers.",question:"Why do we call the Cross a sacrifice?",one:"On the Cross, Christ is the priest who offers and the Lamb who is offered."},
  hook:"Supper anticipates, Cross accomplishes, heaven presents.",
  analogy:{title:"The letter and the signature",text:"A will is written beforehand, signed with a life, and then carried out by an executor. The Supper wrote the testament, the Cross signed it in blood, and the Church, through the Mass, executes it for every generation."}},
 ms3:{plain:{version:"From the Bible and the earliest Christians, the Eucharist was called a sacrifice. The Reformers objected that Christ died once for all. Trent answered: the Mass is not a new sacrifice but the same one made present, with the same priest and victim, offered in a different, unbloody way.",question:"Is the Mass really a sacrifice?",one:"Same priest, same victim, different manner."},
  hook:"Same priest, same victim, different manner (Trent, Session 22, ch. 2).",
  sort:{title:"Defined by Trent, or condemned by Trent?",cats:["Defined","Condemned"],items:[["The Mass is a true and proper sacrifice",0],["The Mass is only a meal",1],["The Mass may be offered for the dead",0],["The Mass takes away from the Cross",1],["The Mass is propitiatory",0]]}},
 ms4:{plain:{version:"A sacrifice needs something done to the victim, but the risen Christ cannot die again. The Church teaches that in the Mass Christ is offered in an unbloody way. The usual explanation is the separate consecration of bread and wine, which shows his body and blood apart, the sign of his death, while the whole Christ is really present.",question:"Where is the 'sacrifice' in the Mass if Christ doesn't die again?",one:"The separate consecration makes present, under signs, the one death of Christ."},
  hook:"Vi verborum: body here, blood there. Concomitance: whole Christ in each.",
  sort:{title:"Which family of explanation?",cats:["Destruction / change of state","Mystical immolation","Oblation","Sacramental sacrifice"],items:[["Bellarmine",0],["De Lugo",0],["Billot",1],["Lessius",1],["De la Taille",2],["Vonier",3]]}},
 ms5:{plain:{version:"After the consecration, what looks like bread and wine is no longer bread and wine but Jesus himself, body, blood, soul and divinity. The appearances stay; what the thing is changes. That is why we adore the Host.",question:"How is Jesus present in the Eucharist?",one:"The whole substance changes; the appearances remain."},
  hook:"Substance changes, accidents remain, the whole Christ in every part.",
  analogy:{title:"The butterfly in reverse",text:"In nature, a caterpillar keeps its identity while its appearance changes completely. In the Eucharist it is the other way round: the appearance stays exactly the same while the identity changes completely."}},
 ms6:{plain:{version:"The Mass has two main parts, the Word and the Eucharist, as on the road to Emmaus. The Eucharistic Prayer is its heart: thanks, the Holy, Holy, the calling down of the Spirit, the consecration, the remembering and offering, prayers for everyone, and the Great Amen.",question:"What is happening at each moment of Mass?",one:"Word, then Eucharist; and the Eucharistic Prayer is the summit."},
  hook:"Thanks · Holy · Spirit · Words · Remember · Offer · Pray · Amen."},
 ms7:{plain:{version:"The Mass is of infinite value, but how much it helps us depends on how we come. Masses can be offered for the living and the dead. The offering given to a priest supports him; it never buys grace. Lay people truly offer the sacrifice and themselves with it.",question:"What does the Mass do for me and for others?",one:"Come with an enlarged heart; offer yourself with Christ."},
  hook:"Infinite fountain, finite cup.",
  sort:{title:"Right or wrong about Mass offerings?",cats:["Right","Wrong"],items:[["Priests should celebrate for the poor without an offering",0],["A bigger stipend buys more grace",1],["The offering supports the priest",0],["Any appearance of trade must be avoided",0],["One Mass can be applied to ten paid intentions separately",1]]}}
});
